[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The program is a single-value oracle: it reads one `float` with `scanf("%f", &x)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and prints the four bytes of that `float`'s object representation as lowercase
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m hex, host byte order, followed by `\n`. stderr is always empty and the exit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m status is always 0, so **every** observable difference is a difference in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m value `scanf` stored — or in whether it stored anything at all.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m That last point drives the test design. `x` is initialized to `0.f`, and on a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m matching failure `scanf` leaves the object untouched, so a failed conversion is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m indistinguishable from a successful conversion of `0`. The two cases separate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m only when the input carries a leading `-`: a failure prints `00000000` (`+0.0f`),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a success prints `00000080` (`-0.0f`). Every failure-path input in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/differential.rs` is therefore also exercised with a `-` prefix, via
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `with_all_prefixes`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `0x` prefix with neither a hex digit nor a `.` must be a matching failure
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Status: fixed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Fixed in: `src/cfloat.rs`, the hex branch of `strtof`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Found by: a randomized token sweep over the float-grammar alphabet with a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   forced `-` prefix (now `random_token_soup`); minimized to `-0x`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Regression test: `hex_prefix_without_digits`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Failing inputs, all of the same shape — `0x`/`0X` followed by something that is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m neither a hex digit nor `.`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input    | C (correct) | Rust (before fix) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | -------- | ----------- | ----------------- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-0x`    | `00000000`  | `00000080`        |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-0X`    | `00000000`  | `00000080`        |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-0xg`   | `00000000`  | `00000080`        |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-0xp1`  | `00000000`  | `00000080`        |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-0x+1`  | `00000000`  | `00000080`        |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-0xz9`  | `00000000`  | `00000080`        |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-0x\n1` | `00000000`  | `00000080`        |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Cause: the translation treated a digitless hex prefix as "`strtof` converts only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the leading `0`", returning a signed zero. That is what a bare
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtof("-0x")` does, but `scanf` is not a bare `strtof`. glibc's `vfscanf`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m collects the token itself and tracks whether it saw a digit or a decimal point
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *after* the `0x` prefix; when it saw neither, the conversion is a matching
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m failure and nothing is stored, so `x` keeps its initial `+0.0f`. The Rust code
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stored `-0.0f` instead, flipping the sign bit.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The neighbouring case must not be broken while fixing this: a collected `.`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m satisfies the "saw a decimal point" condition, the token *is* handed to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtof`, and `strtof` then rejects the hex form and converts just the leading
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0`. So the sign does survive there:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input     | C          | Rust (after fix) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --------- | ---------- | ---------------- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-0x.`    | `00000080` | `00000080`       |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-0x.p1`  | `00000080` | `00000080`       |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-0x.g`   | `00000080` | `00000080`       |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-0x..`   | `00000080` | `00000080`       |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The fix keys on exactly that: after `0x` with no hex digits anywhere, succeed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with a signed zero if the next byte is `.`, otherwise report a matching failure.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note the contrast with the decimal form, where a lone `.` has no leading `0` for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtof` to fall back on: `-.` consumes nothing, so it fails and prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `00000000`. The translation already handled that (`parse_dec` returns `None`),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `matching_failures_leave_the_initial_value` covers it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours confirmed correct (no change needed)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each was probed specifically because it is a plausible place for a translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to drift, and each already matched:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Matching failure vs. `-0`, across every non-numeric leading byte — including
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   all 256 byte values in four positions (`every_byte_value_in_every_position`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `inf`/`infinity`: `vfscanf` accepts only a 3- or 8-character match, so `infi`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `infin`, `infini` and `infinit` are matching failures, not infinity, while
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `infx` and `infinityy` succeed on their 3- and 8-character prefixes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Case-insensitive throughout.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `nan`: the optional `(n-char-sequence)` payload is never collected, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `nan(123)` yields a plain quiet NaN rather than a NaN carrying payload 123.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `-nan` sets the sign bit (`0000c0ff`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Incomplete exponents back off without consuming: `1e` → `1.0`, `1e+` → `1.0`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0x1p` → `1.0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Signed zero for every zero-valued token: `-0`, `-0.`, `-.0`, `-0e999`, `-0x0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Round-to-nearest-ties-to-even at exact decimal midpoints between adjacent
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   binary32 values, including the `+0`/smallest-subnormal tie, the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   subnormal→normal boundary, and the `2^24` integer-spacing boundary.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Overflow to `inf` and underflow to `0`, including the half-ulp-above-`FLT_MAX`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   tie (`3.4028236692093846e38`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Exponent fields that overflow `int` (40-digit exponents, `1e2147483648`), in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   both decimal and hex forms.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Tokens up to 200 000 bytes, and leading-zero runs that must not consume
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   significand precision.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `scanf`'s leading-whitespace skip crossing newlines, and the fact that only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the first conversion runs, so trailing lines are never read.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Empty input, whitespace-only input, `/dev/null` and a closed descriptor on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   stdin all print `00000000`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `argv` is ignored (`main()` takes no parameters).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Coverage
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c_src/src/main.c` has no data-dependent branch of its own: `print_hex` always
